@VisualMelon

> It isn't my lack of experience, it is my reasoning that the type system will allow me to do things I didn't intend. No amount of experience can change that. But yes, perhaps I'd be less afraid if I had more experience (assuming the experience is good).

This is the second time you've stated this, but it doesn't sound right. I've seen the type system do things people don't intend in a variety of ways. For example, which overload is chosen in specific scenarios. Any system that is keyed toward reigning in existing complexity, like generics, combines exponentially with all the language features and expectations that were there before it. You're speaking as if this is not already the kind of concept that competent programmers take in stride.

@jnm2 I shouldn't wish to suggest I've said anything that anybody else hasn't considered, but no-one else has written this stuff down _here_ (which is why you are talking to me and no-one else), and I'm not saying that C# is perfect (it is far from it). Features do indeed combine to give ludicrous complexity (and power), but thus far (in C#) they are all concerned with named types, and not the members inside them. This serves to significantly bound the complexity: the programmer (and language features) only have to reason about named types (excepting value tuples, but my understanding is that they are nicely contained; and 'magic methods', but they too are limited in their influence).

Overloading absolutely creates problems, but it doesn't "reach inside types". The same is true of implicit generic parameters. Implicit conversions are another thing that goes wrong, but they are explicitly defined (someone had to reason that `int -> float` in particular was worth it and write it unambiguously in terms of named types) and don't reach inside types either. When these things go wrong, the intent that is violated is that of the author of the call(s) rather than the author of the type. (I should have made this last bit clear, which I completely failed to do before)

@masaeedu 
[Here (gist, because it's not short)](https://gist.github.com/VisualMelon/171df96392c9525c7806d56bc80066bd) is a really crass example (working C# with annotations). Your comments are spot on: in a way, structural typing is nominal with regard to members, whereas nominal typing is nominal with regard to types. My issue with the proposal is that it mixes a feature which is member centric with a language and framework that are (essentially) type centric.

Your solution to ensuring non-ambiguity surely works (by essentially including the type-name/other-info in the member name, though that symbol tidiness isn't available in C#), but it is working around the rest of the type-system (i.e. simulated nominal typing through structural typing in an otherwise nominal type system), and means more overhead (in code) for everyone. The access control options you point out are intriguing...

