@VisualMelon 

I can only point to what I see in the code examples that there is at least some degree of structural typing.  [For example](https://github.com/MattWindsor91/roslyn/blob/master/concepts/code/ShapesComparison/Program.cs#L76):

```cs
public concept CGroup<T>      // public shape SGroup<T>
{                             // {
    T operator +(T t1, T t2); //     static T operator +(T t1, T t2);
    T Zero { get; }           //     static T Zero { get; }
}                             // }
```

Given that concept/shape, the instance/extension you'd use to allow `int` to be used would be:

```cs
public instance IntGroup : CGroup<int>       // public extension IntGroup of int : SGroup<int>
{                                            // {
    int Zero => 0;                           //     public static int Zero => 0;
}   
```

Which at least means that the `+` operator can be wired up implicitly.

But what if instead of `T Zero { get; }` the member was `T MinValue { get; }`?  Would there be a point to `IntGroup` at all?  What if `Zero` is furnished via a static extension property, which "extension everything intends to allow?

Would we still be required to write:

```cs
public instance IntGroup : CGroup<int> { }   // public extension IntGroup of int : SGroup<int> { }
```

I would hope that the answer is no because frankly it's noise.  The compiler is certainly smart enough to make that association and there's no value to shoe-horning it through a nominal proxy, even if (and especially if) the members are automatically wired up anyway.

I think the "mayhem" about structural typing is way overblown.

